WEBVTT

00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:07.417
An anti-join answers a very practical question: which rows on one side have no related row on the other side?

00:00:07.417 --> 00:00:12.247
The small example shows three patients and only two patients with lab results.

00:00:12.247 --> 00:00:17.997
We first use a LEFT JOIN because we want to preserve every patient.

00:00:17.997 --> 00:00:22.447
After that join, the patient with no lab result has NULL values on the lab side.

00:00:22.447 --> 00:00:27.452
Then we use WHERE and check whether the lab test ID is NULL.

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:30.957
That leaves only the patient with no matching lab row.

00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:34.457
The choice of column for the NULL check matters.

00:00:34.457 --> 00:00:39.817
We should test a right-side key that cannot be NULL for a real matched row.

00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:43.897
A primary key such as test ID is a good choice.

00:00:43.897 --> 00:00:51.777
If we test a column that can legitimately contain NULL, we could mistakenly classify a matched record as

00:00:51.777 --> 00:00:54.902
unmatched. This pattern is useful far beyond healthcare.

00:00:54.902 --> 00:01:01.067
We can find customers with no orders, students with no submission, products with no sales,

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:06.252
employees with no required training, or patients with no follow-up activity.

00:01:06.252 --> 00:01:10.072
Read the logic as a sentence: preserve all patients, try to match lab results,

00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:15.334
then keep only the patients for whom no lab result was found.
